{"title":"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Easier_Way_to_Continue\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Easier Way to Continue</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Even_Easier_Way_to_Continue\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Even Easier Way to Continue</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(with_trig,_not_recommended)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (with trig, not recommended)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3(Easier_Trig)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3(Easier Trig)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(elimination)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (elimination)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(guesstimation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (guesstimation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(using_trig_to_express_related_sides)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (using trig to express related sides)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Rectangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/9/9596b5a2993d0f1ce48e542106bde5b216018861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = 6$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bf318adbc664ea1b3a7b469be11de66bce0de67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is chosen on side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/4/334938df809265ca173a80d6e78381ad6b564998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD = \\angle CMD$\" width=\"147\" height=\"13\" />. What is the degree measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc71a1fff5886a534a1ccd7c0f725e85c1f3dc69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/2/c822379e3c4160d748a6e8ce4d031d0eb2d000c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 15 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 30 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 45 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 60 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 75$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/0/730ddc517474ace5c79ccbe56c72ca8c918276c6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(10mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.5pt)+fontsize(10pt)); dotfactor=3;  pair A=(0,3), B=(6,3), C=(6,0), D=(0,0); pair M=(0.80385,3);  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(M--C); draw(M--D); draw(anglemark(A,M,D)); draw(anglemark(D,M,C));   pair[] ps={A,B,C,D,M}; dot(ps); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,NW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,NE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,SE); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,SW); label(&quot;$M$&quot;,M,N); label(&quot;$6$&quot;,midpoint(C--M),SW); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,midpoint(B--C),E); label(&quot;$6$&quot;,midpoint(C--D),S);  [/asy]\" width=\"325\" height=\"178\" />\n</center>\n<p>It is given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/b/8bb9e171a6dd748e3f8e70576572a10815799033.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD \\sim \\angle CMD$\" width=\"147\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc71a1fff5886a534a1ccd7c0f725e85c1f3dc69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/8/ee8a0fe7b209ee3731073cfeb2efcf7edeb496a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CDM$\" width=\"61\" height=\"12\" /> are alternate interior angles and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f84a7716f13b8bc8ee2750f47cbd26eca19ea532.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{DC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf2d1cd99e8371e649589a7b98650e138fe4916.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD \\cong \\angle CDM \\longrightarrow \\angle CMD \\cong \\angle CDM$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"336\" height=\"13\" />. Use the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Base_Angle_Theorem\" title=\"Base Angle Theorem\">Base Angle Theorem</a> to show <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/8/f985fafb339c9a5114b72ac014a8a08b652ccd2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{DC} \\cong \\overline{MC}$\" width=\"88\" height=\"15\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rectangle\" title=\"Rectangle\">rectangle</a>, so it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/a/e1a22b89860eeca087b61047f5dd196b9d26b25b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{MC} = 6$\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" />. We notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0faed260933ee64e9300b5750492d63184fc7687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BMC$\" width=\"64\" height=\"13\" /> is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/c/47c78b85058ee5e17f88e2cde302e48aad826b35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30-60-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"15\" /> triangle, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7fe901fa76fd9bb89f8239f57abb4d8bf9eb81f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BMC = 30^{\\circ}$\" width=\"109\" height=\"13\" />. If we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/f/f5f4204e536e192d64123bde612c77bc95a806ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" /> then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/1/3d1cba6b42f9bb54a8975c08edc20da47f52a39d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 2x + 30 &amp;= 180\\\\ 2x &amp;= 150\\\\ x &amp;= \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} 75} \\end{align*}\" width=\"141\" height=\"78\" />\n</p><p>Video Explanation: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/IYavr3nEaV4\">https://youtu.be/IYavr3nEaV4</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Easier_Way_to_Continue\">Easier Way to Continue</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Easier Way to Continue\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>After finding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/8/1d85d930b0a88ee62e8e87ee9bcf8bcf071e9002.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MC = 6,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> we can continue using trigonometry as follows. \n</p><p>We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/443c90f5a1348492ed207db75e5557ac02247bae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BMC} = 180-2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"157\" height=\"16\" /> and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/1/ef1a1fbfedc736be52a7f803c8a67242f13becd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin (180-2x) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>It is obvious that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/d/90d27167ff1d43594e189eda0dac1811d0bdf14a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin (30) = \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" /> and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/9/139528d83191398b92cc8f1b2637cacfe70b65ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180-2x=30.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Solving, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/4/8b4fb57c8492160f129342fad73a210b50d1821c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\boxed{75}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p><p>Note: We chose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919bf5d461970fc8d905d63704ef763c08594184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/b/7eb3f65e0ef4165ea603d55d2fce82a817958080.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" /> must be in the first quadrant considering it appears twice and still doesn't cover up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Even_Easier_Way_to_Continue\">Even Easier Way to Continue</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Even Easier Way to Continue\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>After finding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/8/1d85d930b0a88ee62e8e87ee9bcf8bcf071e9002.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MC = 6,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> we can recognize a 30-60-90 from the side lengths of MBC to get angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/b/b0bf8ca99249af2f05178e43c81a58144194cbe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BMC = 30\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"102\" height=\"14\" />\n-HiHiJocky\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28with_trig.2C_not_recommended.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(with_trig,_not_recommended)\">Solution 2 (with trig, not recommended)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (with trig, not recommended)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/183cff37a6482b8b6bbcbfbcce2125a7a6d0ced7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{DMC} = \\angle{AMD} = \\theta$\" width=\"181\" height=\"13\" />. If we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/f/17f86ce0ad24da08336d28ac26230f15c8bb9e72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM = x$\" width=\"67\" height=\"13\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/8/7c8454d14b8337690cf7536c527d3f2ab837daff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MD = \\sqrt{x^2 + 9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />, by the Pythagorean Theorem, and similarily, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/8/8e8331fa4713cf67ca4732b07efc433c76e461ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MC = \\sqrt{x^2 - 12x + 45}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"183\" height=\"18\" />. Applying the law of cosine, we see that \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/e/23edbeb823cfb142915e6bc48aee1f8b06cb5382.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2x^2 + 54 - 12x - 2 \\sqrt{x^4 - 12x^3 + 54x^2 - 108x + 405} \\cdot \\cos (\\theta) = 36\\]\" width=\"479\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/3/ca3b4ce6e8867a1cef5e764648532f952a4b0e81.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\tan (\\theta) = \\frac{3}{x}\\]\" width=\"86\" height=\"37\" /> YAY!!! We have two equations for two variables... that are relatively difficult to deal with. Well, we'll try to solve it. First of all, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/e/e5e6d24645a5b6c82baa8957a028c19a15fef787.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin (\\theta) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2+9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"136\" height=\"42\" />, so solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/5/fa5d77211d57ee91092c4e05114d70e79a2dfc1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos (\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" /> in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe530fdb19f5ef5afb8b54a9b07773ef00b8362.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos (\\theta) = \\frac{x \\cdot \\sqrt{x^2 + 9}}{x^2 + 9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"162\" height=\"42\" />. The equation now becomes \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/b/b0bb7d3bb8cb794a979f1c29a1c118ae49f86904.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2x^2 + 54 - 12x - 2 \\sqrt{x^4 - 12x^3 + 54x^2 - 108x + 405} \\cdot \\frac{x \\cdot \\sqrt{x^2 + 9}}{x^2 + 9} = 36\\]\" width=\"522\" height=\"42\" />\nSimplifying, we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/f/9df95979146d72cdefbcffc0f29aa3b02ad92b1a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[4x^4 - 48x^3 + 216x^2 - 432x + 324\\]\" width=\"261\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Now, we apply the quartic formula to get \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/4/5b4510fcca1f74476d75ee687beff8e614815f5e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x = 6 \\pm 3 \\sqrt{3}\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We can easily see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/0/a607775f999d66a06e55233f4522c4b2a0c1e30e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 6 + 3 \\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"21\" /> is an invalid solution. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/b/9cbb796d26e6f222f2ba117ba66f1001e1c33fa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 6 - 3 \\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>Finally, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/d/c8d37a1ecaf51d5341f994a68aeb5abf93a95763.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan (\\theta) = \\frac{3}{6 - 3 \\sqrt{3}} = 2 + \\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"222\" height=\"42\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/2/8c2b086833669a574325fac8bafb08c3d75cdf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = \\frac{5 + 12n}{12} \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"38\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is any integer. Converting to degrees, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/f/24ff4ee03bdf3db856af07feeb001256517a3d19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = 75 + 180n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"111\" height=\"14\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/f/e0fba87ee0dbac4f781c4ae80591cd1ebdb367c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; \\theta &lt; 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"84\" height=\"13\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/9/959841eca8d3d3a0140f42c1076d391b4a1624bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = \\boxed{75}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"23\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p>~ilovepi3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3.28Easier_Trig.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3(Easier_Trig)\">Solution 3(Easier Trig)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3(Easier Trig)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/d/50d8db13a2f61273ced07258fd814e429c8c8ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC=CM=6$\" width=\"121\" height=\"12\" />. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/4/234d0cffac77674915e641d26da2a5120ffb65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM=\\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=3\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"188\" height=\"21\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/609974dedcc918d80ff4c0641dc3bee123e04363.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=6-3\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"122\" height=\"18\" />, We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc7b627f3fad214b4079e3fe8d0f7cb4fd734d4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan(AMD)=\\frac{6-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}=2+\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"262\" height=\"41\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/2/0325e288e9015e36d194374ff2be316cbb8afa88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD=\\boxed{75}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"117\" height=\"25\" />\n~awsomek\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28elimination.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(elimination)\">Solution 4 (elimination)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (elimination)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/7/307d851b726b591c18a041d410e214fe9ba295b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AMD=\\angle DMC=\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"183\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb6aa28922f22138afc5e1a2d9ca4b9336322a45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BMC=180-2\\theta\\implies\\angle MCB=2\\theta-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"349\" height=\"16\" />. Since all angles should be positive, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/a/aca0f490f2ea53cc2bdf1f7120cb17ab88939cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta&gt;45^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"16\" />, narrowing the options to D and E. Trying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> (option D), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/9/a79261daa583cc78a1b13a23ac574de93d7a86df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta AMD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" /> is a 30-60-90 triangle. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/8/6e867467073fae9550c977a21ee2cb19426c4f92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD=3$\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />, so it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/0/28048bcb0d91f97f05b75d665d9622e8c5019451.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"21\" />. \nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/6/166c791e5ec2185f9a6f3d677184f493f925ff65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BMC=180-2\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"155\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/f/0af85f5cf1427ce20235ed5691be47643d13734c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BMC=60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"113\" height=\"15\" />, too. However, that would imply that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/a/bba461d22ed8a6975810f662fcb8a57023f4b854.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta MBC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" /> is also a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/c/47c78b85058ee5e17f88e2cde302e48aad826b35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30-60-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"15\" /> triangle, which would, in turn, imply that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744626bfc15bd2fe8511fd2865a502111d4323e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MB=3\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"21\" />, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6680a35b22c11c0ee9f16a760de124151579b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=3$\" width=\"61\" height=\"12\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee64880085a34e628fb1a52c9c1450b59499fd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM+MB=AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"141\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/0/21032de0a62898b5a7c21d1d8be4ba7bf4c8252a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=6,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> but we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/0/28048bcb0d91f97f05b75d665d9622e8c5019451.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744626bfc15bd2fe8511fd2865a502111d4323e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MB=3\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"21\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d906a73acb776afea69cea31f52aa65845c8bdfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM+MB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"92\" height=\"16\" /> is clearly not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so this is not possible. \nThus, the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/e/d2eba879aaaaec80939575434d09d7cc69bb5c70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~75}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"64\" height=\"30\" />.\n~ Technodoggo\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28guesstimation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(guesstimation)\">Solution 5 (guesstimation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (guesstimation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We draw a diagram. It seems that they are larger than 60 degrees. We try 75, and with the knowledge that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f882c445127c216a71826e800d8d5aaeabe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{sin}(75^\\circ)\\approx 0.96$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/d/b4df599fda054cb9ca7aeca5ce63eb8060f2f9f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cos}(75^\\circ)\\approx 0.26$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" />, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/8/8a8c29248fa44d2c9416620e5e8ce3355de40085.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cot}(75^\\circ)\\approx \\frac{4}{15}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" />, and this gives us missing side lengths of 0.8, 5.2, 5.9, and 3.1, which happens to satisfy all equations. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/1/3711217e5f5d573cdedc2efeb489063c5d6ed51e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{E}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"24\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28using_trig_to_express_related_sides.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(using_trig_to_express_related_sides)\">Solution 6 (using trig to express related sides)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (using trig to express related sides)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let \\(\\angle AMD=\\angle CMD=x\\). Then, \\(\\angle BMC=180-2x\\). \n\\(\\tan (x)=\\frac{AD}{AM}=\\frac{3}{AM}\\). Thus, we have \\(AM=\\frac{3}{\\tan (x)}\\). Similarly, \\(\\tan (180-2x)=\\frac{BC}{BM}=\\frac{3}{BM}\\). Thus, we have \\(BM=\\frac{3}{\\tan (180-2x)}\\). Using the fact that \\(\\tan (180-2x)=-\\tan (2x)\\), we can write \\(BM\\) as \\(\\frac{-3}{\\tan (2x)}\\). \nSince \\(AM+BM=6\\), we have \\(\\frac{3}{\\tan (x)}-\\frac{3}{\\tan (2x)}=6\\). \\(\\tan (2x)=\\frac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan ^{2}x}\\), so \\(\\frac{3}{\\tan (x)}-\\frac{3(1-\\tan ^{2}x)}{2\\tan (x)}=6\\). Multiplying both sides by \\(2\\tan (x)\\), we have \\(6-3+3\\tan ^{2}(x)=12\\tan (x)\\). Therefore, \\(\\tan ^{2}(x)-4\\tan (x)+1=0\\). Solving the quadratic, we have \\(\\tan (x)=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\). If \\(\\tan (x)=2-\\sqrt{3}\\), then \\(AM=\\frac{3}{2-\\sqrt{3}}=6+3\\sqrt{3}\\), which is larger than the side length of \\(6\\). Therefore, \\(\\tan (x)=2+\\sqrt{3}\\), and since \\(x\\) is between \\(0\\) and \\(180\\) degrees, \\(x=75\\) degrees.\n~Spiritmoon_OSU\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Invisible Angle Chasing - <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/IYavr3nEaV4\">https://youtu.be/IYavr3nEaV4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B\">2011 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">Problem 19</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017224102\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.058 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.076 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 547/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2324/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 158/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16283/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.073      1 -total\n 59.95%    7.837      1 Template:AMC10_box\n 39.05%    5.105      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:9093:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017224102 and revision id 290944. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}